    <string-array name="reading_passage_titles">
        <item>The North Wind and the Sun</item>
        <item>Separation of Tasks</item>
        <item>Social Time</item>
        <item>The Dichotomy of Control</item>
        <item>Flow</item>
        <item>Sunk Cost</item>
    </string-array>
    <string-array name="reading_passage_texts">
        <item>The North Wind and the Sun were arguing over which of them was stronger. A traveler wrapped in a cloak came along the road. They agreed that whoever could make the traveler remove the cloak would be considered the stronger. The North Wind went first. It gathered all its force and blew fiercely at the traveler. But the harder it blew, the more tightly the traveler pulled the cloak around himself. The North Wind grew tired and finally fell still. Then the Sun came out from behind the clouds and first shone gently. Feeling the warmth, the traveler loosened the cloak. The Sun rose higher, the air grew hot, and at last the traveler took off the cloak himself and sat down to rest in the shade. The North Wind had to admit that the Sun was stronger. Gentleness and patience can sometimes achieve more than force.</item>
        <item>Adlerian psychology divides problems in relationships into “my tasks” and “other people’s tasks.” How I speak, whether I am honest, and which consequences I am willing to accept are my tasks. Whether another person understands, agrees, or changes is their task. Separation of tasks is not indifference, nor is it a refusal to care. It means not making choices for others and not allowing their judgments to take over my life. Clear boundaries can make it easier to meet people as equals, with greater stability and respect.</item>
        <item>Social time is not the even flow of physical time measured by a clock. It is a rhythm and order shaped collectively by a group. Holidays, work cycles, life stages, and generational memory all give time meaning. Through institutions and customs, a society also tells people when they should study, work, or start a family, subtly shaping our sense of “too early” and “too late.” Seeing these arrangements does not require rejecting every rule. It helps us distinguish rhythms that genuinely fit us from timetables written on our behalf by others.</item>
        <item>Stoic philosophy divides things into two categories: what is within our control and what is not. Judgment, choice, and effort belong to the first; other people’s opinions, facts that have already happened, the weather, and final outcomes belong to the second. Much suffering comes from pouring energy into what we cannot control while neglecting the place where we can act. The dichotomy of control is not about abandoning goals. It means doing our part and then returning what is not ours to the world. We can still act with care without turning every outcome into a verdict on ourselves.</item>
        <item>Flow is a state of deep involvement in which people lose track of time and of excessive self-consciousness. It tends to appear in activities with clear goals, prompt feedback, and a challenge that roughly matches one’s ability. Tasks that are too easy create boredom, while tasks that are too difficult create anxiety. When the challenge is slightly beyond the current level of skill, attention gathers naturally around the work itself. Flow is not a demand to be productive at every moment. It is the discovery of a sustainable rhythm in which concentration becomes part of the experience.</item>
        <item>A sunk cost is time, money, or emotion that has already been spent and cannot be recovered. Rationally, whether to continue or stop should depend only on future benefits and costs. Yet people often refuse to leave because they have already invested so much. The more we cling to the past, the easier it becomes to keep adding to it. Recognizing a sunk cost does not deny that the earlier effort was sincere. It acknowledges that the value of some pursuits has come to an end. The hardest choice is often not starting over, but allowing ourselves to stop.</item>
    </string-array>
